# Postmortem: The Two Record Versions Were Not Merged

During the first check, the entry point for adj-i18n showed the "current revision" for two revisions together. The memory sample differed from the old record, but at the time it was impossible to confirm whether the entry content had changed or whether another object had been read. The two artifacts had similar names, and the entry point showed only one current location, so it was easy to mistake the situation for an ordinary update.

The first step was not to organize the results, but to preserve the original input and the entry-point version. The original is kept in `docs/adj-i18n/incidents/i18n-dependency-boundary-audit-two-version-records-not-merged.md`; further retries were stopped because new output might overwrite the difference observed the first time. The old record was not turned into the new record here, and the two revisions were not combined under one name.

Only a few things could be confirmed from the incident:

- The memory sample differed from the old record;
- The current records for two revisions appeared at the same entry point;
- The binary payload was later recoverable;
- It is still unknown why Protobuf changed;
- It is also unknown whether the two revisions cover the same set of adopters.

Recovery cannot be treated as the root cause. The binary payload being readable only shows that at least one path became available again; it cannot explain why the entry point carried two versions of the record at the same time, much less show that the Protobuf change has disappeared. The original entry point remains the starting point for rechecking, and the failure window is retained in the record.

Missing timestamp: ____

The subsequent handoff added the version, request context, and stop conditions to avoid looking only at the last line of green output. When following this path again, first verify whether the input still points to the same object, then decide whether to continue; actions that were not recorded are not filled in as speculation.
